`BookingReferenceRepository.module.ts` imports `BookingReferenceRepository` from `@calcom/lib/server/repository/bookingReference`. That path does not exist in the repo: `packages/lib/server/repository/` has no `bookingReference` file or directory. The class actually lives in `@calcom/features/bookingReference/repositories/BookingReferenceRepository`, which is what `handleCancelBooking.ts` imports.

The module is pulled in by `BookingCancelService.module.ts`, so any code that loads the cancel-service DI container fails at import time, not only the cancellation path.

::code{file="packages/features/bookings/di/BookingReferenceRepository.module.ts" lines="1-5"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The type checker and the tests that load the container will both fail on this import. The fix is a one-line import change.

::suggestion{file="packages/features/bookings/di/BookingReferenceRepository.module.ts" lines="3-3"}
import { BookingReferenceRepository } from "@calcom/features/bookingReference/repositories/BookingReferenceRepository";

The PR removes `locale: true` from the shared `userSelect` in `UserRepository.ts`. The organizer is now loaded with `userRepository.findByIdOrThrow`, which calls `findById` and uses that `userSelect`. `handleCancelBooking.ts` still reads `organizer.locale`, both to build `tOrganizer` and in the `language` field of the organizer's calendar event.

The result is twofold. The type checker reports `locale` as missing from the return type. At runtime `organizer.locale` is `undefined`, so `?? "en"` makes every organizer-facing cancellation email and calendar event render in English, even for users whose locale is set. Removing `locale` from a shared select also affects every other caller of `UserRepository.findById`, which the PR does not address.

::diff{file="packages/features/bookings/lib/handleCancelBooking.ts" lines="336-338"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The old code selected `locale` inline in the handler. The PR removed it from the shared select without a replacement, so the new `findByIdOrThrow` call returns a user without `locale`.

Fix by adding `locale: true` back to the shared `userSelect` in `UserRepository.ts`, or by selecting it in the organizer lookup. Check other `findById` callers before choosing.

In the recurring branch, the `updateMany` that cancels instances uses `gte = cancelSubsequentBookings ? bookingToDelete.startTime : new Date()`. The follow-up `findMany` that collects `allUpdatedBookings` used to use `gte: new Date()`. The PR changes it to `gte: bookingToDelete.startTime` with no explanation.

That means for `allRemainingBookings` (where `cancelSubsequentBookings` is false), the `findMany` now returns instances that started between `bookingToDelete.startTime` and now but were never cancelled. Those instances then go into `onBulkBookingsCancelled` as CANCELLED, have their workflow reminders deleted, and are sent in webhook and audit payloads. The filter only matches the `updateMany` in the `cancelSubsequentBookings` case.

The PR's own review notes ask that the query filters match the original. This one does not, and no test covers it.

::diff{file="packages/features/bookings/lib/handleCancelBooking.ts" lines="513-521"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The two queries should use the same predicate. Either compute `gte` once and reuse it for both calls, or restore `new Date()` for the `findMany` if that was the intent. If the intent is to fix the `cancelSubsequentBookings` case, say so in the PR description and add a test for it.

`IBookingRepository.updateMany` is declared as `Promise<{ count: number }>`. The implementation in `BookingRepository.ts` is `async updateMany(...) { await this.prismaClient.booking.updateMany(...) }` with no `return`, so it resolves to `Promise<void>`.

Because `BookingRepository` declares `implements IBookingRepository`, TypeScript should reject this. The method is also at column 0 instead of the class indentation, which points to a bad merge edit.

::diff{file="packages/features/bookings/repositories/BookingRepository.ts" lines="1502-1508"}

::callout{type="info"}
The handler ignores the return value, so the fix is to return the Prisma result. Keep the interface as written, or change it to `Promise<void>` if the count isn't needed. Either way the two must agree.

The new `PrismaBookingAttendeeRepository.ts` uses single quotes and omits semicolons, unlike the rest of the package and the repo formatter. Its class name also matches the API v2 wrapper and the `platform/libraries` export, so `import` sites need aliases to tell them apart.

`BookingCancelService.module.ts` mixes a relative import (`../lib/handleCancelBooking`) with aliased `@calcom` imports, and places `DI_TOKENS` after them. The `handleCancelBooking.ts` diff also re-indents the `team` spread block and `ProfileRepository.ts` re-indents unrelated object literals, which adds review noise without behavior change.

